  "worked_solution": "Since both fractions are over a common denominator, we can add them. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta $ to simplify the expression in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
